<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Zaragoza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1529">1529</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Saragossa, Capitulation of Zaragoza</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> was a peace treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Portugal, signed on 22 April 1529 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> III of Portugal and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in the Aragonese city of Zaragoza. It settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a> issue, which arose in 1520 when both kingdoms claimed the Spice Islands as lying within their zones under the 1494 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a>. The treaty set the border 297½ leagues, or 17°, east of the Maluku Islands, leaving them to Portugal, which paid Charles V 350,000 gold ducats. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> were not mentioned, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> later established a trading post at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> in 1565.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> of 1494 set a meridian in the Atlantic, with areas to the west exclusive to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and those to the east to Portugal, but the precise line was never established and each side interpreted it in the most favourable light. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1511. Learning the location of the Spice Islands, the Bandas and Ternate and Tidore in the Malukus, then the single source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a>, he sent an expedition under António de Abreu, which arrived in early 1512. Abreu's lieutenant Francisco Serrão obtained a licence at Ternate to build a Portuguese factory and fortress.</p><p>Letters from Serrão helped his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan</a> persuade the Spanish crown to finance a voyage round the world. On 6 November 1521 Magellan's fleet, now under Juan Sebastián Elcano, reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a> from the east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> then sent García Jofre de Loaísa to colonise the islands. The Spanish built a fort on Tidore, there was conflict with the Portuguese on Ternate, and nearly a decade of skirmishes followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1524 both kingdoms organised the Junta de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a>–Elvas. Each crown appointed three astronomers and cartographers, three pilots and three mathematicians, to fix the antimeridian of Tordesillas, with the aim of dividing the world into two equal hemispheres. The committee met several times without agreement, since geographic knowledge was inadequate to assign longitude accurately and each group chose maps or globes that put the islands in its own hemisphere.</p><p>Marriages eased the way. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>'s sister Catherine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> married John III on 10 February 1525, and on 11 March 1526 Charles married John's sister Isabella of Portugal. It was in the emperor's interest to avoid conflict so as to concentrate on European policy, and the Spaniards did not yet know how to carry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> from the Malukus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by the eastern route.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty fixed the eastern boundary between the two zones at 297½ leagues, or 1,763 kilometres, or 17°, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Maluku Islands</a>, leaving them within the Portuguese domain, in exchange for 350,000 gold ducats paid to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. A safeguard clause allowed the emperor to undo the deal by reimbursing the money, but he needed the Portuguese payment to finance the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-league-of-cognac">War of the League of Cognac</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a>. The treaty did not clarify the Tordesillas line or validate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s claim to equal hemispheres of 180° each, so the two lines divided the Earth into unequal portions: about 191° for Portugal and about 169° for Spain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Zaragoza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
